// The proof of `number_does_pre_and_post_is_constant` — the contract claim:
// either the precondition fails, or the answer satisfies the postcondition.
//
// This used to be the corpus's hardest proof: a page of hand-derived cuts,
// because its then arm is reached only when `is_tag` held — a fact that is
// a *disjunction* (`v = t1` or `v = t2`) no rule window could spend, so the
// case analysis had to be written out waypoint by waypoint. The diagram
// engine builds that case analysis itself: branches become an ordered case
// tree, the arm that tested equal to a literal holds the literal, and each
// leaf folds on the machine. What the proof has left to say is the one
// thing the engine never does on its own — spend the definitions — and
// which of the four `equal`s each split is about: the tag tests are named
// by their literals, since an unaddressed `cases(equal)` takes the first
// it is offered, which is the postcondition's own shape guard and decides
// nothing.
proof types_test::number_does_pre_and_post_is_constant =
    inline both(decide)
    cases(equal(types_test::t1)) both(decide)
    cases(equal(types_test::t2)) both(decide)
    diagram;
